<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Cell Biol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">J. Cell Biol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">jcellbiol</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>The Journal of Cell Biology</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0021-9525</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-8140</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">6736124</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2275617</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">84239970</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Articles</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Phosphorylation of microtubule-associated proteins by aCa2+/calmodulin- dependent protein kinase</article-title></title-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>7</month><year>1984</year></pub-date><volume>99</volume><issue>1 Pt 1</issue><fpage>11</fpage><lpage>19</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1984, </copyright-statement><copyright-year>1984</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>In an earlier study I demonstrated that rat brain cytosol contains aCa2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase activity that phosphorylatesmicrotubule-associated protein 2 (MAP-2) but not MAP-1. Comparison of sitesof phosphate incorporated in MAP-2 catalyzed by theCa2+/calmodulin-dependent kinase activity and the cyclic AMP-dependentprotein kinase activity in cytosolic extracts revealed distinct sites ofphosphorylation (Schulman, H., 1984, Mol. Cell. Biol., 4:1175-1178;abstract by me and J.A. Kuret and K.H. Spitzer [1983, Fed. Proc., 42:2250].I have now used MAP-2 as a substrate to purify theCa2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase responsible for MAP-2phosphorylation. The brain appears to contain a single predominantCa2+/calmodulin-dependent protein kinase that phosphorylates MAP-2. Theenzyme was purified to apparent homogeneity by column chromatography usingDEAE-cellulose, phosphocellulose, hydroxylapatite, Sepharose 6B, and acalmodulin-Sepharose affinity column. The 580,000-dalton holoenzymeconsists of 51,000- and 60,000-dalton subunits. The purified enzymephosphorylates MAP-2 at the same "sites" that are phosphorylated incytosolic extracts and thus has the same specificity as the activitypresent in cytosol. Analysis of phosphorylated MAP-2.1 and MAP-2.2, the twocomponents of MAP-2, suggests considerable homology in their phosphorylateddomains.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>